Article 15

Prevention of violence against women is the responsibility of:
a. Regional Government;
b. educational institutions;
c. religious institutions;
d. health service provider institutions; e. professional institutions;
f. business sector;
g. service institutions;
h. Islamic boarding schools;
i. youth organizations;
j. legal aid organizations;
k. communities; andl. media.

Article 17

1. Prevention of violence against women is carried out in the following fields: a. education;
b. health;
c. religion;
d. security;
e. justice;
f. public works and spatial planning;
g. public housing and settlement areas; h. social affairs;
i. labor;
j. women's empowerment and child protection; k. land;
l. environment;
m. population administration and civil registration; n. population control and family planning; o. transportation;
p. communication and informatics;
q. cooperatives, small and medium enterprises;
r. youth and sports;
s. culture;
t. tourism;
u. trade and industry; and v. other related fields.
2. Prevention of violence against women as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out in the following forms: a. developing media for communication, information, education and public campaigns through media;
b. developing educational materials and curricula; c. developing safe transportation systems and public spaces;
d. establishing early detection systems, security and integrated complaint services in industrial areas, companies, residential areas, educational environments, Islamic boarding schools and other public spaces;
e. providing consultation services for families, prospective brides and grooms, students, Islamic boarding school students, youth, university students and vulnerable groups;
f. encouraging behavioral change of parents and the community;
g. developing policies related to regencies/cities, villages, schools, Islamic boarding schools, and universities that are safe and friendly for women and integrated with regional spatial planning policies;
h. establishing and developing cadres, communities, and groups from among youth, students, Islamic boarding school students, university students, journalists, social media influencers, educators, and religious leaders for prevention;
i. conducting education and advocacy to owners, managers and/or users of social media;
j. developing protection systems and special support for vulnerable groups of women;
k. conducting awareness-raising for perpetrators;
l. conducting cooperation among those responsible for prevention of violence against women; and m. conducting socialization of laws and regulations. (3) The fields and forms of prevention of violence against women as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) are the responsibility of Regional Apparatus that oversee related affairs.

Article 24

1. Forms of services as referred to in Article 23 include: a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. social rehabilitation services;
d. legal aid and law enforcement services; and e. repatriation and social reintegration services. (2) Services for women victims of violence are implemented quickly, free of charge, comprehensively, with quality and integrated with services provided by government agencies, the community, educational institutions, religious institutions, professional institutions, Islamic boarding schools, the business sector through an integrated service system.

Article 21

P2TP2A as referred to in Article 20 consists of the following elements:
a. related regional apparatus;
b. government and/or private hospitals;
c. police;
d. prosecutor's office;
e. courts;
f. regional office of the ministry of law and human rights;
g. regional office of the ministry of religious affairs;
h. professional organizations;
i. religious organizations;
j. community social organizations;
k. government-owned service institutions and community-owned service institutions;
l. legal aid organizations;
m. women's organizations;
n. universities; and o. broadcasting media and/or mass media.

Article 20

1. In implementing protection services for women, the Regional Government establishes P2TP2A as a service network for women victims of violence.
2. The establishment of P2TP2A is intended so that women victims of violence obtain comprehensive, integrated, quality, inclusive and sustainable services.
3. P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) has the following duties: a. providing services to women victims of violence in accordance with standard operating procedures; b. coordinating prevention, services and referrals among government-owned service institutions, community-owned service institutions, professional organizations, law enforcement agencies and other related institutions;
c. conducting data collection on case services;
d. providing various information needed by victims, victims' families and the community;
e. conducting prevention of recurrence of violence, exploitation and discrimination against women; f. conducting monitoring and evaluation of victim services; and g. providing capacity strengthening, guidance and assistance related to victim services.

Article 14

1. Strengthening and development of women's empowerment service provider institutions shall be implemented by the Regional Apparatus that handles governmental affairs in the field of women's empowerment.
2. Strengthening and development of women's empowerment service provider institutions as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:a. socialization;
b. coordination;
c. communication, information and education;
d. training; ande. increasing participation of community members.

Article 23

1. Services are implemented to fulfill the rights of women victims of violence and are carried out with the following principles: a. respect for human rights; b. non-discrimination;
c. confidentiality;
d. empathy;
e. inclusion;
f. empowerment;
g. victim-based decisions;
h. honesty;
i. professionalism;
j. integration; and k. sustainability.
2. Services to women victims of violence must consider special conditions and needs based on the vulnerability experienced by women as referred to in Article 20

Article 29

1. Repatriation and social reintegration services as referred to in Article 24 paragraph (1) letter e shall be implemented with the following activities:a. repatriation and/or pick-up of victims; b. provision of population documents or personal identity;
c. alternative family;
d. scholarships and educational support facilities; e. non-formal education;
f. skills training and economic enterprise;
g. financial assistance and business capital;
h. economic enterprise assistance;
i. housing assistance;
j. food and/or nutrition assistance for victims and their families;
k. provision of social protection programs and poverty alleviation programs;
l. other forms of repatriation and social reintegration services needed by victims.
2. Repatriation and social reintegration services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be provided to victims, families and persons whose lives depend on victims with the aim that victims have the readiness and ability to live in society.
3. Repatriation and social reintegration services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out in an integrated manner with the Integrated Referral Service System for social protection and poverty alleviation and the Child Social Welfare Center.
4. Repatriation and social reintegration services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be the responsibility of the Regional Apparatus in charge of:a. social affairs;
b. population administration and civil registration affairs;
c. labor affairs;
d. cooperatives, small and medium enterprises affairs; e. education affairs;
f. health affairs;
g. public works and spatial planning affairs; h. land affairs, women's empowerment and child protection affairs; andi. other related affairs.
5. Further provisions regarding repatriation and social reintegration services shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 38

1. The Regional Government undertakes efforts to strengthen and develop service provider institutions including: a. guidance;
b. counseling;
c. supervision;
d. facilitation of family development implementation; and e. improving the health quality of pregnant and breastfeeding mothers.

Article 30

1. Strengthening of women's protection service provider institutions through the establishment of community shelters in every sub-district and village;
2. Human resource development for women's protection service provider institutions for shelter members that have been formed through training.

Article 4

1. To improve the quality of life of women, the Regional Government is responsible for:a. respecting and guaranteeing women's rights without discrimination in accordance with human dignity;
b. institutionalization of Gender Mainstreaming in Regional Government institutions; c. Women's Empowerment;
d. strengthening and developing Women's Empowerment service provider institutions in the Region.
2. Efforts to improve the quality of life of women as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by Regional Apparatus that administer governmental affairs in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.

Article 27

1. Social rehabilitation services as referred to in Article 24 paragraph (1) letter c shall be implemented with the following activities:a. counseling;
b. assistance;
c. linguists and/or translators;
d. spiritual guidance;
e. shelter and/or safe house;
f. temporary shelter;
g. strengthening based on family and/or group support;
h. mobility support for victims with disabilities;
i. provision of special social assistance for victims; and j. other forms of social rehabilitation services needed by victims.
2. Social rehabilitation services as referred to in paragraph (1) letters a, b, c, d, f, g, h, and i shall be the responsibility of the Regional Apparatus in charge of women's empowerment and child protection affairs.
3. Social rehabilitation services as referred to in paragraph (1) letters e and j shall be the responsibility of the Regional Apparatus in charge of social affairs and women's empowerment and child protection affairs.
4. Further provisions regarding social rehabilitation services shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

